    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1094">

                <pb facs="1120" n="1106" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.01/11–144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stewart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-01T16:00:00-06:00">November 1, 1944—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:12 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>638. Referring to my telegram No. 637 dated October 31, 6 p.m.<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d1094-f1"/> An aide to President Somoza informed me this morning
                    that the President recognizes that his recognition of the new government in <pb
                        facs="1121" n="1107" xml:id="pg1107"/> El Salvador was premature and a
                    mistake. He confirmed that the action was taken following the receipt of an
                    urgent request from the Mcaraguan Minister in El Salvador.<anchor n="46"
                        xml:id="d1094-f2"/> In part, however, it was due to the President’s absence
                    on Lake Nicaragua off Granada and to misunderstanding of messages transmitted to
                    him by Dr. Argüello the Foreign Minister who is now blamed for the affair. The
                    aide said that President Somoza would accept any suggestions we might make to
                    him in the premises and if we so desired would find some way to negate the
                    recognition.</p>
                <p>Repeated to El Salvador.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stewart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
